
  #93 -- can p, beta or homeostasis move the arc's 2:1 trade?
  n=1000 k=50, 60 sentences, 2 seeds, NEURON IDS throughout
  a real conjunction needs BOTH separations below 0.5

              cell     p  beta  homeo  across-STATE  across-MOOD   multi  1-mood
          baseline  0.05  0.06  False          0.98         0.81    4/16     0/4
            p=0.10  0.10  0.06  False          0.97         0.89    2/16     0/4
    p=0.20 (paper)  0.20  0.06  False          0.95         0.91    2/16     0/4
       homeostasis  0.05  0.06   True          1.00         0.46    1/16     0/4
    p=0.20 + homeo  0.20  0.06   True          1.00         0.61    2/16     2/4
        + low beta  0.20  0.01   True          0.99         0.62    2/16     0/4

  READING

    P1 raising p lowers across-STATE:         True   0.98 -> 0.95
    P2 homeostasis moves the RATIO:          False   best homeo cell 1.00 / 0.46
    P3 some cell has BOTH below 0.5:         False   none

    best sum: homeostasis -> 1.00 + 0.46 = 1.46   (baseline 1.79), multi 1/16, floor 0/4

    NO CELL IS A CONJUNCTION. Across every axis on which our
    setup violates the theorem -- density, plasticity, per-round
    homeostasis -- one shared k-WTA area still cannot hold
    (state, symbol). The remedy is then architectural, not
    parametric: an arc area per state, or the paper's mutual
    inhibition over the ROLE areas, which this repo has measured
    as never once firing ([[mutual-inhibition-prefers-untrained]]).

    WARNING: a cell that improved the separations FAILED the
    single-mood floor. Improvement there is not a better
    conjunction, it is a model that stopped working -- the
    failure mode that made the #95 arc arms uninterpretable.
